# TypeScript Conventions

[This is an example rules file. Replace the content with conventions observed in your actual codebase. Run Prompt 2 from the starter pack to auto-generate rules from your code.]

## Stack
- TypeScript 5.x with `strict: true`
- Node.js 18+, ES2022 target
- Vitest for testing, tsc for type checking

## Directory Structure (IDesign Layered)

```
src/
├── contracts/           # Interfaces, types, DTOs (leaf — zero cross-layer imports)
├── engine/              # Pure business logic (no I/O, no DA calls)
├── data_access/         # Platform adapters (database queries, API calls)
├── utility/             # Cross-cutting helpers
├── infrastructure/      # DI, composition roots, test doubles
└── __tests__/
    ├── unit/            # Pure logic tests
    └── component/       # Tests with test doubles
```

## Import Rules

| Layer | May import from |
|-------|----------------|
| `contracts/` | Nothing (leaf) |
| `engine/` | `contracts/` only |
| `utility/` | `contracts/` only |
| `data_access/` | `contracts/`, `utility/` |
| `infrastructure/` | `contracts/` |
| `__tests__/` | Any layer (test code is exempt) |

## Do
- Use `import type` for type-only imports
- Use `interface` for object shapes, `type` for unions/aliases
- Export through barrel files (`index.ts`) at each layer directory
- Keep engine functions pure: `(data) → result`, no side effects
- Use `null` for absent values (not `undefined`)
- Run `npm run typecheck && npm test` before considering work complete

## Don't
- Use `any` type — use proper typed interfaces
- Use `enum` — use union string literal types
- Co-locate an interface and its implementation in the same file
- Put business logic in `data_access/`
- Import from `engine/` or `data_access/` inside `contracts/`
